    "durationMs": 253334,
    "finalText": "Implementation complete. The URL shortener is built and fully verified:\n\n**Stack**: NestJS 11.2.11 + TypeScript 5.8.3, TypeORM (`@nestjs/typeorm` — Nest's idiomatic persistence layer) with the `pg` driver, reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment.\n\n**What I verified with a live HTTP test suite:**\n- `POST /links` → 201 with generated or custom codes; submitted URLs stored unchanged; duplicate custom code → 409\n- `GET /links/{code}` → 200 / 404; `GET /links` → `{\"items\", \"total\"}` in ascending creation order\n- `GET /r/{code}` → 302 with correct `Location`, one atomic increment per hit (`increment()` = single `UPDATE ... clicks + 1`)\n- 422 with `{\"error\"}` for malformed JSON (custom body-parser error middleware), missing/relative/`ftp`/`http:/`/`http:///` URLs, and codes failing `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`\n- **Persistence**: killed and restarted via `sh start.sh` — clicks, links, and duplicate-code checks all survived; second run reports \"No pending migrations\" (idempotent)\n- Server binds `0.0.0.0:8000` (confirmed via `/proc/net/tcp`), port freed and `links` table truncated so the automated suite starts clean\n\nOne notable bug caught during testing: `http:///path` initially passed validation because WHATWG's URL parser skips extra slashes after special schemes — fixed with a negative-lookahead requiring a non-empty authority.\n\n`start.sh` applies migrations then serves in the foreground (no dependency installation), and the README documents startup. Nothing was committed to git, per convention."
  }
